WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.940
Hello everyone! Welcome to the LangFocus channel, 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.000
Today we are going to talk about 
the language of pigeons.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.260
Not funny, bro.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.700
Sorry, I know that joke isn't very good,
 but that's all I've got.

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:19.500
Today, I'm going to talk about some special kind 
of languages, called Pidgins and Creoles.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.540
You've probably heard thoses terms before.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.820
Maybe in the names of certain languages,
like Pidgin English or Haitian Creole,

00:00:25.899 --> 00:00:30.759
but please note, there is not just a single 
pidgin language or a single creole language.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.700
They are actually refering to categories of languages.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.000
There are many pidgins 
and many creoles around the world.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.799
First, what do they have in common?

00:00:38.799 --> 00:00:42.199
Well, Pidgins and Creoles 
are new languages that developped

00:00:42.299 --> 00:00:47.159
when speakers of different languages come into contact 
with each other and have a need to communicate.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.799
Many Pidgins and Creoles  have arisen  when 
colonial powers came into contact with local people,

00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:54.659
as they spread around the world.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:57.500
So, what is the difference between Pidgins and Creoles?

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.440
Well, Pidgins are non native lingua francas
while Creoles have native speakers.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.100
But let me get into both types of languages.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:05.600
Okay

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.240
First of all, pidgin languages.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:10.400
As I just mentioned,
pidgin languages have no native speakers.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.599
They are languages 
that arise very suddenly, very quickly,

00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:16.799
when there is a need of  communication 
in a certain situation.

00:01:16.799 --> 00:01:19.439
For example, for trade or for labour.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.020
The Pidgin language is a sort of compromise between 
two different languages or between multiple languages.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:29.900
Pidgins usually arise when one group of people is 
dominant over another group of people,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.500
and the less dominant group needs to learn 
to communicate with the more dominant group.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.599
But, without any formal education 
in the language of that dominant group,

00:01:38.599 --> 00:01:44.779
they adopt a sort of simplified language based on the 
most basic vocabulary of that dominant group's language

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.940
and based on the most basic grammar 
of their own native language.

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:54.599
This happens most commonly in situations 
of trade, slavery or colonial contact.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:02:01.180
And in a situation like that, the dominant group is usually 
the colonial power or the most influential trading partner.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.200
Imagine this situation: an English-speaking 
colonial power has a sugar plantation

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.000
and they bring over labourers from various 
different countries to that sugar plantation

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.960
and those people speak different languages.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:16.300
But, in order to work together,
they need to have a common language.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.980
So they try to adopt English, but since they do this quickly,
and they just adopt the basics,

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:27.160
the resulting language is a very simplified language, 
just using the basic vocabulary of English

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.800
and a very non English grammar.
That is a Pidgin language.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:33.000
Got it!

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:36.820
When Pidgins first developped, 
they are typically restricted in use.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.780
Meaning that they are only used for a certain purpose.
For example, on the job or for trade.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.599
But some Pidgins become expanded Pidgins, meaning
that they begin to be used for all facettes of life

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:52.899
including social life, family life, things like that,
and they become languages in their own rights.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.520
Even though they are not native languages,
they are passed down from generation to generation

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:01.800
as lingua francas among people
who speak different native languages

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.800
Now about Creole languages.

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:06.120
So, Pidgins have no native speakers.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.940
They arise because the need for a lingua franca,

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:13.800
but if that language survives and becomes 
the native language of the next generation,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.000
then it is now a Creole language.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:21.199
For example, slaves from several different countries 
are working closely together on a plantation,

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:23.000
but they have no common language.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:26.900
So, they develop their Pidgin 
and it quickly becomes their expanded Pidgin,

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.800
the language they use for all everyday purposes.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:35.080
Their children grow up in that environment with 
that Pidgin language and it becomes their native language.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.900
That is now a Creole language.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.240
In the days when African slaves 
were brought to the Americas,

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.000
they were often separated from people 
who spoke the same native language.

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.699
That was to prevent them from rebelling 
or from making plans together, that kind of things.

00:03:48.699 --> 00:03:53.199
So, in those kinds of situations, 
the Pidgin languages became expanded very quickly

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:56.399
and, within one generation, 
sometimes became Creoles.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:59.900
Some Creoles are based on English.
For example, Jamaican Creole.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.699
Some Creoles are based on French,

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:05.179
Including Haitian Creole, 
which has 12 million native speakers.

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.340
And the smaller number of Creole languages 
are based on Spanish,

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.300
including Chavacano, in the Philippines.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:14.200
Now, how different are these Creole languages 
from their parent languages?

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.640
Well, it depends of the particular language; 
but let's look at an example.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:22.600
This example is from Bislamá, which is 
an English-based Creole spoken in Vanuatu.

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.100
The first sentence is "This is my house"

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.600
In Bislamá: "Hem ya haos blong mi".

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.899
Or in more Standard English phonology : 
"Him here house belong me"

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.600
Another example sentence: 
"I have already been to town"

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:38.200
In Bislamá: "Mi bin long taon finis".

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:43.000
Or in Standard English phonology :
"Me been long town finish"

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.000
So look at each word : "Mi" is like "I", 
"bin", that's self-explanatory.

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.440
"long" is used as a preposition here instead 
of an adjective. So "long" means "towards" or "to"

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:55.600
And then, we have "taon", that is like "town"

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.500
and "finis". "finis" is used instead of "already"
to show that something has been accomplished.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.300
So, again you can see that the words are from English

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.000
but they are used and arranged in a very different way,

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.399
and this is what makes it a different language
than the parent language.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:14.300
Pidgins and Creoles are fascinating because they are
proof that languages are living entities,

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.399
that are constantly changing and adapting 
to the needs of their speakers.

00:05:18.399 --> 00:05:24.799
Sometimes, two or more languages can even join forces
and take on a life of their own as a new language.

00:05:24.799 --> 00:05:25.859
Thank you for watching.

00:05:25.859 --> 00:05:28.119
Be sure to leave your comments down below.

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:30.219
And have a nice day.

